      "id": 1,
      "text": "Poplar became the tribal headquarters for the Fort Peck Indian Reservation after the military abandoned Camp Poplar in 1893.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poplar%2C_Montana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Poplar River joins the Missouri River near town, forming a confluence that early settlers used for trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poplar%2C_Montana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lewis and Clark camped near the Poplar River on May 3, 1805, about 3.5 miles upstream from its mouth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://missouririvermt.com/community/poplar",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town hosts an annual Native American Pow Wow called Poplar Indian Days every first weekend in September, drawing dancers and drummers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poplar%2C_Montana",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Poplar Museum and Tribal Museum showcase tribal history and artifacts, preserving the heritage of the Assiniboine and Sioux tribes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
